            <ArticleTitle>Metabolic factors in diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis - a review of clinical data.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>116-28</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2174/1874312901408010116</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We aimed to review the literature linking metabolic factors to Diffuse Idiopathic Skeletal Hyperostosis (DISH), in order to assess associations between growth factors and DISH.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">We identified studies in our personal database and PubMed using the following keywords in various combinations: &quot;diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis&quot;, &quot;ankylosing hyperostosis&quot;, &quot;Forestier's disease&quot;, &quot;diabetes&quot;, &quot;insulin&quot;, &quot;obesity&quot;, &quot;metabolic&quot;, &quot;growth factors&quot;, &quot;adipokines&quot;, &quot;glucose tolerance&quot; and &quot;chondrocytes&quot;.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We were not able to do a systematic review due to variability in methodology of studies. We found positive associations between obesity (especially abdominal obesity), Type 2 diabetes mellitus, glucose intolerance, hyperinsulinemia and DISH.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Current research indicates that certain metabolic factors associate with DISH. More precise studies deriving from these findings on these and other newly identified bone-growth factors are needed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
